00:00:00.020 --> 00:00:04.660
Most computer vision tasks could use more data and so data

00:00:04.660 --> 00:00:09.060
augmentation is one of the techniques that is often used to improve the

00:00:09.060 --> 00:00:14.260
performance of computer vision systems. I think that computer vision is a pretty

00:00:14.260 --> 00:00:19.220
complicated task. You have to input this image, all these pixels, and then figure

00:00:19.220 --> 00:00:24.220
out what is in this picture. And it seems like you need to learn a decently

00:00:24.220 --> 00:00:29.660
complicated function to do that. And in practice, for almost all computer vision

00:00:29.660 --> 00:00:34.580
tasks, having more data will help. This is unlike some other domains where

00:00:34.580 --> 00:00:38.620
sometimes you can get enough data that you don't feel as much pressure to get even

00:00:38.620 --> 00:00:42.620
more data. But I think today the state of computer vision is that for the

00:00:42.620 --> 00:00:46.740
majority of computer vision problems, we feel like we just can't get enough data.

00:00:46.740 --> 00:00:51.060
And this is not true for all applications of machine learning, but it

00:00:51.060 --> 00:00:55.180
does feel like it's true for computer vision. So what that means is that when

00:00:55.180 --> 00:01:00.100
you're training computer vision model, often data augmentation will help. And

00:01:00.100 --> 00:01:03.580
this is true whether you're using transfer learning and using someone else's

00:01:03.580 --> 00:01:07.260
pre trained ways to start, or whether you're trying to train something

00:01:07.420 --> 00:01:11.820
yourself from scratch. Let's take a look at the common data augmentation

00:01:11.820 --> 00:01:17.220
methods in computer vision. Perhaps the simplest data augmentation method is

00:01:17.220 --> 00:01:21.940
mirroring on the vertical axis, where if you have this example in your

00:01:21.940 --> 00:01:27.940
training set, you flip it horizontally to get that image on the right. And for most

00:01:27.940 --> 00:01:33.140
computer vision tasks, if the left picture is a cat, then mirroring it is still a cat.

00:01:33.780 --> 00:01:38.260
And if the mirroring operation preserves whatever you're trying to recognize in

00:01:38.260 --> 00:01:44.340
the picture, this would be a good data augmentation technique to use. Another

00:01:44.340 --> 00:01:49.180
commonly used technique is random cropping. So given this data set, let's pick a

00:01:49.180 --> 00:01:54.460
few random crops. So you might pick that and take that crop. You might take that,

00:01:54.780 --> 00:02:00.420
take that crop, take this, take that crop. And so this gives you different

00:02:00.420 --> 00:02:04.460
examples to feed into your training set with sort of different random crops of

00:02:04.460 --> 00:02:09.740
your data set. So random cropping isn't a perfect data augmentation method. You know,

00:02:09.740 --> 00:02:14.820
one of you randomly end up taking that crop, right, which won't look much like a

00:02:14.820 --> 00:02:19.700
cat. But in practice, it works well, so long as your random crops are reasonably

00:02:19.700 --> 00:02:24.220
large subsets of the original image. So mirroring and random cropping are

00:02:24.220 --> 00:02:29.420
frequently used. And in theory, you could also use things like rotation,

00:02:29.620 --> 00:02:35.100
shearing of the image. So that's if you do this to the image, distorted that

00:02:35.100 --> 00:02:39.980
way, introduce various forms of local warping, and so on. And there's really

00:02:39.980 --> 00:02:43.460
no harm with trying all of these things as well, although in practice,

00:02:43.460 --> 00:02:52.780
they seem to be used a bit less, perhaps because of the complexity. A second type

00:02:52.820 --> 00:02:59.820
of data augmentation that is commonly used is color shifting. So given a

00:02:59.820 --> 00:03:08.140
picture like this, let's say you add to the R, G, and B channels, different

00:03:08.300 --> 00:03:14.060
distortions. In this example, we are adding to the red and blue channels

00:03:14.060 --> 00:03:20.740
and subtracting from the green channel. So red and blue make purple. So this

00:03:20.740 --> 00:03:25.300
makes the whole image a bit more purpley. And that creates a distorted image

00:03:25.500 --> 00:03:30.380
for your training set. For illustration purposes, I'm making somewhat dramatic

00:03:30.380 --> 00:03:34.740
changes to the colors. In practice, you draw R, G, and B from some

00:03:34.740 --> 00:03:41.740
distribution that could be quite small as well. But what you do is take

00:03:41.740 --> 00:03:47.100
different values of R, G, and B and use them to distort the color channels. So in

00:03:47.100 --> 00:03:52.660
the second example, we are making a less red and more green and more blue. So that

00:03:52.660 --> 00:04:00.250
turns the whole image a bit more yellowish. And here we are making it much

00:04:00.250 --> 00:04:04.930
more blue, just a tiny little bit more red. But in practice, the values R, G,

00:04:04.930 --> 00:04:11.900
and B are drawn from some probability distribution. And the motivation for

00:04:11.900 --> 00:04:17.700
this is that if maybe the sunlight was a bit yellower, or maybe the indoor

00:04:17.700 --> 00:04:23.060
illumination was a bit more yellow, that could easily change the color of an

00:04:23.060 --> 00:04:28.820
image, but the identity of the cat or the identity of the content, the label Y,

00:04:28.980 --> 00:04:33.980
should still say the same. And so introducing these color distortions, or

00:04:34.020 --> 00:04:39.220
by doing color shifting, this makes your learning algorithm more robust to

00:04:39.220 --> 00:04:52.300
changes in the colors of your images. Just a comment for the advanced learners

00:04:52.340 --> 00:04:58.580
in this course. And it's okay if you don't understand what I'm about to say

00:04:58.580 --> 00:05:04.460
when I'm using in red. There are different ways to sample R, G, and B. One

00:05:04.460 --> 00:05:08.660
of the ways to implement color distortion uses an algorithm called PCA.

00:05:08.740 --> 00:05:14.300
This is called principles components analysis, which I talked about in the

00:05:14.340 --> 00:05:22.060
machine learning book in the ML class.org machine learning course on course

00:05:22.060 --> 00:05:27.900
error. But the details of this are actually given in the AlexNet paper,

00:05:28.260 --> 00:05:37.660
and it's called sometimes called PCA color augmentation. But the rough idea

00:05:37.660 --> 00:05:43.420
between PCA color augmentation is, for example, if your image is mainly

00:05:43.420 --> 00:05:50.420
purple, if it mainly has red and blue tints, and very little green, then PCA

00:05:50.420 --> 00:05:55.340
color augmentation will add and subtract a lot to red and blue, but relatively

00:05:55.340 --> 00:05:59.900
little to green. So it kind of keeps the overall color of the tint the

00:05:59.900 --> 00:06:05.980
same. If you didn't understand any of this, don't worry about it. But if

00:06:05.980 --> 00:06:11.820
you can search online for, but you can if you want to read about the details

00:06:11.820 --> 00:06:15.700
of it in the AlexNet paper. And you can also find some open source

00:06:15.700 --> 00:06:23.100
implementations of PCA color augmentation, and just use that. So you

00:06:23.100 --> 00:06:27.740
might have your data stored, your training data stored in a hard disk

00:06:27.740 --> 00:06:32.140
when they use this symbol, this round bucket symbol to represent your

00:06:32.140 --> 00:06:36.700
hard disk. And if you have a small training set, you could do almost

00:06:36.700 --> 00:06:40.300
anything and they'll be okay. But if you're very large training set, then

00:06:40.300 --> 00:06:44.540
this is how people will often implement it, which is you might have a CPU

00:06:44.540 --> 00:06:54.620
thread that is constantly loading images off your hard disk. So you have

00:06:54.620 --> 00:07:01.690
this, you know, stream of images coming in from your hard disk. And what

00:07:01.690 --> 00:07:09.770
you can do is use maybe a CPU thread to implement the distortions, be it the

00:07:09.770 --> 00:07:17.260
random cropping or the color shifting or the mirroring. But for each image, you

00:07:17.260 --> 00:07:22.900
might then end up with some distorted version of it. So let's see this image,

00:07:22.900 --> 00:07:27.020
I'm gonna mirror it and maybe also implement color distortion and so on.

00:07:27.020 --> 00:07:39.310
And maybe this image ends up being color shifted. So you end up with, you

00:07:39.310 --> 00:07:45.790
know, some different color cat. And so your CPU thread is constantly loading

00:07:45.790 --> 00:07:52.190
data, as well as implementing whatever distortions are needed to form a batch or

00:07:52.190 --> 00:08:02.260
really mini batches of data. And this data is then constantly passed to some

00:08:02.260 --> 00:08:06.460
other thread or some other process for implementing training. And this could

00:08:06.460 --> 00:08:11.140
be done on the CPU or really increasingly on the GPU if you have a

00:08:11.140 --> 00:08:18.100
large neural network to train. And so a pretty common way of implementing data

00:08:18.100 --> 00:08:26.510
augmentation is to really have, you know, one thread or multiple threads that is

00:08:26.510 --> 00:08:31.510
responsible for loading the data and implementing distortions, and then passing

00:08:31.510 --> 00:08:35.950
that to some other thread or some other process that then does the training. And

00:08:35.950 --> 00:08:44.500
often this and this can run in parallel. So that's it for data

00:08:44.500 --> 00:08:50.220
augmentation. And similar to other parts of training a deep neural network, the

00:08:50.220 --> 00:08:55.380
data augmentation process also has a few hyper parameters such as how much color

00:08:55.380 --> 00:09:00.420
shifting do you implement and exactly what parameters you use for random cropping.

00:09:00.420 --> 00:09:05.460
So similar to elsewhere in computer vision, a good place to get started might

00:09:05.460 --> 00:09:10.660
be to use someone else's open source implementation for how they use data

00:09:10.660 --> 00:09:15.900
augmentation. But of course, if you want to capture more in variances than you

00:09:15.900 --> 00:09:20.700
think someone else's open source implementation isn't, it might be reasonable

00:09:20.700 --> 00:09:26.420
to also tune these hyper parameters yourself. So with that, I hope that you'll be able to

00:09:26.420 --> 00:09:30.780
use data augmentation to get your computer vision applications to work better.
